EXT. SLED DOG RACE - MIDPOINT CHECKPOINT - NIGHT

A frozen lake. Wind carves the snow into long, sharp ridges. A canvas tent glows orange at the shore, lit by a generator whose hum is swallowed by the dark. Beyond it, forty sled dogs lie curled in straw beds, steam rising off their backs.

A battered radio on a folding table crackles. A hand-lettered sign flaps from a pole: "CHECKPOINT 6 - 480 MILES TO GO."

NILA KOYUK (30s, musher, parka stiff with ice, goggles pushed up) stands at the edge of the straw, counting dogs under her breath. Eleven. Twelve. Thirteen. She stops. Counts again.

DR. HESTER VANCE (50s, race veterinarian, round glasses fogged white) kneels beside a sleek husky named Biscuit. She runs her gloved fingers along the dog's foreleg.

VANCE
Easy, girl. Easy.

NILA
How's the wrist?

VANCE
She's favoring it. Not broken. Not good, either.

NILA
She's my lead dog, Doc.

VANCE
Then she shouldn't lead tonight.

Nila crouches. The wind pushes at her hood. She takes Biscuit's face in both hands. The dog licks her chin.

NILA
Hey, Biscuit. Hey. You tired?

The dog thumps her tail. Nila exhales, long and slow.

The radio crackles. RACE MARSHAL (V.O.) comes through, flat with distance.

RACE MARSHAL (V.O.)
(over radio)
Checkpoint Six, this is Marshal. Weather's closing from the north. Teams have a two-hour window.

NILA
Copy, Marshal. Two hours.

RACE MARSHAL (V.O.)
(over radio)
Koyuk, you're in third. Chandler is forty minutes ahead.

Nila's jaw tightens. Vance glances up.

VANCE
Forty minutes isn't far.

NILA
It's far enough.

She stands. Brushes snow from her knees. Looks across the lake, where nothing but white and darkness meets.

NILA (CONT'D)
Doc, what would you do?

VANCE
I'd scratch.

NILA
I mean really.

VANCE
Really? I'd scratch.

Nila laughs, short and bitter.

NILA
That's not very sporting.

VANCE
I'm a vet. I treat the ones who can't speak. You know what she's saying right now.

Both women look at Biscuit. The dog limps one step, then another, then stops and looks back at Nila, ears forward.

NILA
She wants to go.

VANCE
She wants to please you. There's a difference.

Silence. The generator coughs. The wind rises.

Nila walks to the sled. Checks the brush bow. Runs a hand along the runner. Doesn't pick up the snow hook.

NILA
(to herself)
Two hours.

She turns back.

NILA (CONT'D)
Doc, give me a second lead. Anyone.

VANCE
Pepper's strong. Not fast.

NILA
Strong is fine.

Vance stands and whistles. A gray dog bounds out of the straw, tail whipping.

VANCE
Pepper. You're promoted.

NILA
(to the dog)
Don't let me down.

Pepper barks once, bright and sure.

The radio crackles again.

RACE MARSHAL (V.O.)
(over radio)
Koyuk, confirm you're rolling?

Nila lifts the handset. Looks at Vance. Vance gives the smallest nod.

NILA
(into the radio)
Rolling, Marshal. Give me a green flag.

She hangs up. Pulls her goggles down.

NILA (CONT'D)
Doc, if it goes bad--

VANCE
Then I'll be here with a heater and a flask.

NILA
Sounds like a plan.

VANCE
It's the only one I've got.

Nila steps onto the runners. Biscuit watches her go from the straw. Her tail slowly sinks.

Vance kneels beside the old lead dog.

VANCE (CONT'D)
(whispering)
She'll be back, girl.

The sled lurches. Thirteen dogs pull into the blue dark. The tent shrinks to a single orange spark, then nothing.

Vance stays kneeling, one hand on Biscuit's flank, until the radio crackles with the next team.
